WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.335
These storefronts are
American companies

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.937
that have had to leave Russia.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:06.506
They now operate
under very similar branding

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.775
and sell very similar products.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.610
But ultimately,
they're not Starbucks

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.445
or Dunkin' Donuts.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.714
The mass exodus
of foreign brands

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.449
and life under sanctions

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.119
have changed things
for the Russian consumer.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.087
A lot of people are
now looking to China

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.390
for goods they used to
get from the West.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.559
In fact, it's so significant
that Xiaomi Corporation

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:30.463
became the number 1 bestseller
of smartphones in 2022,

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.465
dethroning Samsung.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:36.069
Viktoriya and her sister
are a good example

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.105
of people looking to
alternative supply lines.

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.340
They're selling neoprene vests

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.109
after companies like
Billabong left the market.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.912
They identified a company
in Guangdong province

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:47.547
and are now selling
to a growing cohort

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.617
of water sports fans in Russia.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:55.617
The sanctions applied towards
Russia by many suppliers,

00:00:56.423 --> 00:01:01.423
both European and American,
have created opportunities

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:06.566
for Russians to start
their own businesses.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:10.036
It's more local and small
scale at the moment

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:15.036
but we plan to use this
opportunity and not let it go.

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.677
Some people compared
the current environment

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.813
to the Soviet Union
and its isolation,

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:22.549
but in reality it bears much
more resemblance to

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.217
Russia in the 1990s,

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.986
when the collapse of the
centralized communist system

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.155
led to gaping holes
in supply chains

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.457
and left it to ordinary people

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:31.658
to fill in the gaps.

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.860 position:66%
An example from modern day Russia includes
a veto a listings website that's now

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:34.527
Avito,

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.296
a listings website that's now

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:37.497
full of people offering to bring

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:40.366
foreign branded goods
to Russia from abroad.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:43.303
In another example, one
businessman is exporting gold

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.305
from Russia to
United Arab Emirates

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:46.739
in exchange for goods.

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.808
But not every sector
is open to opportunism.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.244
In aviation, airlines are
having to ground planes

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.979
and cannibalize them for parts.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:55.315
And used foreign cars
are selling for more

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.749
than when they were brand new.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:57.917
And for the Kremlin

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.886
that's navigating a
declining economy,

00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:02.622
Viktoriya's entrepreneurship
is encouraged.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.624
In fact, they're calling
this new era

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.426
the mobilization economy.

